# Configuration lives in .env

Application configuration is read from the environment. Do not hardcode it in source.

This includes names and labels, RPC URLs, chain ids, private keys, contract addresses the app chooses, durations, payment-token choice, and feature flags.

```typescript
// Bad
const LABEL = "horrortube";
const RPC = "https://ethereum-sepolia-rpc.publicnode.com";

// Good
const label = process.env.ENS_LABEL;
if (label === undefined || label.trim() === "") {
  throw new Error("ENS_LABEL is required. Set it in .env. See .env.example.");
}
```

- Add every variable to `.env.example` with an empty value. Do not put secrets or real defaults there.
- If a variable is missing or blank, fail with an error that names the variable and points at `.env.example`.
- `.env` stays gitignored. Never commit it.

# Workspace reset

`.env` is not in git. A fresh checkout does not have it. Create `.env` from `.env.example`. The comment directly above each variable tells you how to fill it:

- `# 1password: op://...` — run `op read` on that path once and write the value into `.env`.
- A comment that no 1Password item was found — leave the value empty until the task needs it. If the task needs it, stop and say which variable has no path.

Later commands read `.env`. They do not call 1Password again for variables already written there.

Checked-in contract artifacts are not environment config. ABIs and the pinned deployment address table under `packages/ens/scripts/pin/` and `packages/ens/scripts/abis.ts` stay in the repo. The app still chooses which deployment, label, key, and RPC to use through `.env`.
